Causal State Estimation with Physics-Constrained LLM Reasoning for Cryogenic Propellant Transfer:

Mathematical Foundations and Algorithms

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI : chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini, Grok XAi 

Abstract

We present a mathematical foundation for a diagnostic architecture that couples physics-based state estimation with a causal state representation and a physics-constrained large language model (LLM) reasoning layer, applied to cryogenic propellant transfer. The contribution is theoretical: we establish a function-observability condition for the vapor fraction under regime-dependent sensor configurations, verify the hypotheses of moving horizon estimation (MHE) convergence for the reduced-order model including a persistency-of-excitation condition for joint state–parameter estimation, provide a regularized particle filter with bounded importance weights, and formulate the linear stochastic differential equation (SDE) identifiability condition under which the causal graph is recoverable. We introduce an information-theoretic compression hypothesis, stated as an empirically testable proposition rather than a theorem, and we formalize a safety envelope for the LLM layer based on rejection to the physical posterior. Every result is classified as proved, cited, or hypothesized. No empirical claims are made. The central scientific hypothesis — that the LLM layer adds measurable diagnostic value beyond the non-LLM envelope — is isolated and formulated as a falsifiable prediction to be tested in a subsequent experimental program.

Keywords: cryogenic propellant transfer, function observability, moving horizon estimation, persistency of excitation, particle filtering, causal identifiability, LLM safety envelope, information-theoretic compression.

