Chapter 2. Notation and Standing Assumptions

2.1 Notation

Let (Ω, ℱ, ℙ) be a probability space. We use:

· ℝⁿ: Euclidean space with norm ‖·‖.
· L²(ℝⁿ; μ): square-integrable functions w.r.t. μ.
· B(ℝⁿ): bounded Borel-measurable functions, ‖f‖∞ = sup_x |f(x)|.
· 𝒩(μ, Σ): Gaussian distribution.
· D_KL(p‖q): Kullback–Leibler divergence.
· I(X; Y): mutual information; H(X): Shannon entropy.
· L_f h: Lie derivative of h along f.
· 𝒪(x): observability codistribution at x.

2.2 Standing Assumptions

The model satisfies:

· (S1) Regularity. f, h, σ are C¹; f is Lipschitz with constant L_f, h with constant L_h.
· (S2) Boundedness. Trajectories remain in a compact set 𝒦 ⊂ 𝒳 for admissible inputs.
· (S3) Nondegeneracy. R ≻ 0, Q ⪰ 0; the pair (f, h) has no invariant zero dynamics in the operating region.
· (S4) Physical realizability. P > 0, T > 0, m ≥ 0, α ∈ [0, 1].
· (S5) Bounded model error. The reduced-order model f̃ satisfies ‖f − f̃‖∞ ≤ δ_f on 𝒦 × 𝒰.

