Chapter 3. Cryogenic Transfer State-Space Model

3.1 State and Measurement

The reduced state is

x = [P₁, T₁, m₁, α₁, P₂, T₂, m₂, α₂, ṁ₁₂, Q_leak, a]ᵀ ∈ ℝ¹¹, (3.1)

where subscripts 1, 2 denote supply and receiver tanks, α is vapor fraction (quality), ṁ₁₂ is inter-tank mass flow, Q_leak is parasitic heat leak, and a is vehicle acceleration magnitude.

The measurement vector is

y = [P₁, T₁, P₂, T₂, ṁ₁₂]ᵀ ∈ ℝ⁵. (3.2)

3.2 Lumped Conservation Laws

Mass conservation for node i ∈ {1, 2}:

dm_i/dt = Σ_j ṁ_{ji} − Σ_j ṁ_{ij}. (3.3)

Energy conservation (lumped single-node):

d(m_i u_i)/dt = Σ_j ṁ_{ji} h_j − Σ_j ṁ_{ij} h_i + Q_i. (3.4)

Momentum conservation (quasi-steady branch 1 → 2):

(P₁ − P₂)A₁₂ + ρ g V cos θ / g_c − K_f ṁ₁₂|ṁ₁₂| / (2 ρ A₁₂²) = 0. (3.5)

Vapor fraction dynamics with phase-change rate Γ_evap,i:

dα_i/dt = (Γ_evap,i − α_i ṁ_net,i) / m_i. (3.6)

Heat leak is modeled as a slowly varying parameter: Q̇_leak = 0 + w_Q.

3.3 Equation of State

We write the equation of state generically as P_i = ℰ(m_i, T_i, α_i, V_i). Two regimes are distinguished:

· Single-phase (subcooled or superheated): ∂ℰ/∂α_i |_{m_i, T_i} ≠ 0.
· Saturated two-phase: P_i = P_sat(T_i), so ∂ℰ/∂α_i |_{m_i, T_i} = 0.

The distinction drives the observability result of Chapter 4.

