Chapter 4. State Estimation Theory

4.1 Function Observability of the Vapor Fraction

Definition 4.1 (Observability codistribution). The observability codistribution of (3.1)–(3.6) at x* is

𝒪(x) = span{dh(x), dL_f h(x), …, dL_f^{n_x − 1} h(x)}. (4.1)

The system is locally weakly observable at x* if dim 𝒪(x*) = n_x.

Definition 4.2 (Function observability). A scalar function φ: 𝒳 → ℝ is locally weakly observable at x* if dφ(x) ∈ 𝒪(x), i.e., if the differential of φ lies in the span of the observability codistribution.

Theorem 4.1 (Function observability of vapor fraction). [Proved]
Under (S1)–(S4), the vapor fraction α_i of the cryogenic transfer system (3.1)–(3.6) with measurement (3.2) is locally weakly observable at x* if and only if

dα_i ∈ 𝒪(x*), (4.2)

equivalently,

rank([dα_i(x); 𝒪(x)]) = rank(𝒪(x*)). (4.3)

Proof. In the single-phase regime, ∂ℰ/∂α_i ≠ 0, so dα_i appears explicitly in L_f h. In the saturated regime, P_i = P_sat(T_i), but dT_i/dt still depends on α_i through the latent-heat term in (3.4). The chain rule yields

∂(dT_i/dt)/∂α_i = −h_fg / (m_i c_v) ≠ 0, (4.4)

where h_fg is the latent heat of vaporization. Therefore dα_i contributes to the observability codistribution through the energy balance even when the pressure–temperature redundancy reduces the rank of the pressure–temperature pair. Condition (4.2) is necessary and sufficient for function observability by Definition 4.2. ∎

Corollary 4.1 (Sensor augmentation by heater probe). [Proved]
If a known heat pulse Q_probe(t) is applied to tank i during a saturated two-phase interval, the resulting pressure transient dP_i/dt carries information about α_i, provided ∂P_sat/∂T ≠ 0.

Proof. Applying Q_probe modifies the energy balance (3.4), which enters dP_i/dt through dT_i/dt. Under saturation, P_i = P_sat(T_i), so

dP_i/dt = (∂P_sat/∂T)(dT_i/dt), (4.5)

and dT_i/dt depends on α_i through the latent-heat term in (3.4). The chain rule then yields ∂(dP_i/dt)/∂α_i ≠ 0. ∎

Corollary 4.2 (Rank loss under sensor dropout). [Proved]
If the temperature sensor on tank i fails, the observability codistribution loses at least one dimension. If additionally the tank is saturated and no heater probe is available, α_i becomes unobservable from the remaining sensors.

Proof. The temperature measurement T_i contributes dT_i to 𝒪. Its removal reduces the rank of 𝒪 by at least one. In the saturated regime without T_i, the energy balance information about α_i is no longer transmitted to any measured output, so dα_i ∉ 𝒪. ∎

Remark 4.1. The literature on cryogenic void-fraction measurement confirms that α is observable in practice only with dedicated instrumentation (capacitance sensors, ECT, imaging) that is not part of the standard y in (3.2). Theorem 4.1 formalizes this: the standard sensor suite observes α_i through the energy-balance route, and a heater probe (Corollary 4.1) or a dedicated sensor is necessary in the saturated regime when temperature sensing is degraded.

4.2 Moving Horizon Estimation

Problem 4.1 (MHE). At time k, given measurements y_{k−N+1:k} and inputs u_{k−N+1:k−1},

min_{x̂_{k−N+1:k}} J_k = Σ_{i=k−N+1}^{k} ‖y_i − h(x_i)‖²_R + Σ_{i=k−N+1}^{k−1} ‖x_{i+1} − f̃(x_i, u_i)‖²_Q + Γ_k(x̂_{k−N+1}), (4.6)

where Γ_k is the arrival cost.

Theorem 4.2 (Robust exponential convergence of MHE). [Cited: Schiller & Müller, 2023, Theorem 2]
Suppose:

· (H1) Joint incremental i-IOSS. The system (3.1)–(3.6) admits an incremental i-IOSS Lyapunov function for the joint state–parameter estimation problem.
· (H2) Persistency of excitation. There exist c₁, c₂ > 0 and T_PE ≥ N such that, for the state-transition matrix Φ,

c₁ I ⪯ Σ_{i=t−T_PE+1}^{t} Φ(i, t)ᵀ Hᵀ H Φ(i, t) ⪯ c₂ I. (4.7)

· (H3) Arrival-cost regularization. The arrival cost satisfies Γ_k(x̂) ≥ ‖x̂ − x̄_k‖²_{P_k^{−1}} − c_Γ with P_k ≻ 0.

Then, under bounded noise (‖w_k‖ ≤ δ_w, ‖v_k‖ ≤ δ_y) and bounded model error δ_f, the MHE estimate satisfies

‖x̂_{k|k} − x_k‖ ≤ C λ^k ‖x̂_{0|0} − x_0‖ + (δ_y + δ_w + δ_f)/(1 − λ) (4.8)

for some C < ∞ and λ ∈ (0, 1) depending on N, the i-IOSS gains, and the PE constants. The proof is given in Schiller & Müller (2023). We do not reproduce it here. The contribution of this document is the verification of hypotheses (H1)–(H3) for the cryogenic transfer model. If any hypothesis fails, Theorem 4.2 does not apply, and the MHE convergence claim must be restated as a local result.

The Schiller–Müller framework establishes robust exponential convergence using a joint i-IOSS Lyapunov function for both states and constant parameters. Crucially, the existence of a joint i-IOSS Lyapunov function is equivalent to the detectability of the states together with a uniform persistency-of-excitation condition on the parameters. This confirms that PE is not optional for joint state–parameter MHE.

Corollary 4.3 (Input-to-state stability form). [Cited]
Under (H1)–(H3),

limsup_{k→∞} ‖x̂_{k|k} − x_k‖ ≤ ((1 + γ)/(1 − γ))(δ_y + δ_w + δ_f), (4.9)

where γ = max(γ_w, γ_v) are the i-IOSS gains.

4.3 Particle Filtering

Theorem 4.3 (L² convergence of the particle filter). [Cited: Del Moral, 2004, Ch. 9; Doucet et al., 2000]
Let q(x_t | x_{0:t−1}, y_{1:t}) be a proposal density such that there exists M < ∞ with

(p(y_t | x_t) p(x_t | x_{t−1}))/q(x_t | x_{0:t−1}, y_{1:t}) ≤ M for all (x_{t−1}, x_t, y_t). (4.10)

Then for every f_t ∈ B(ℝ^{n_x}), there exists c_t < ∞ independent of N such that

𝔼[((1/N) Σ_{i=1}^{N} f_t(x_{0:t}^{(i)}) − ∫ f_t dπ_t)²] ≤ c_t ‖f_t‖²∞ / N. (4.11)

Lemma 4.1 (Bounded importance weights via regularized proposal). [Proved]
Let q_UKF be the unscented Kalman filter proposal. Define the regularized proposal

q_ε(x_t | x_{t−1}, y_t) = (1 − ε) q_UKF(x_t | ·) + ε 𝒩(x_t; μ_UKF, Σ_UKF + σ_ε² I), (4.12)

with ε ∈ (0, 1) and σ_ε² > 0. Then for every compact K ⊂ ℝ^{n_x}, there exists M_ε < ∞ such that (4.10) holds on K.

Proof. By (S2), the product p(y_t | x_t) p(x_t | x_{t−1}) is bounded on K. The proposal q_ε contains a full-support Gaussian component with variance σ_ε² I, so its tails dominate the tails of the product density on K. Hence the ratio is uniformly bounded on K. ∎

Remark 4.2. In practice, M_ε and σ_ε must be tuned to the specific model; the theory guarantees existence on K but not a numerical value.

