Chapter 5. Causal Structure Theory

5.1 Dynamic Structural Causal Models

Definition 5.1 (DSCM). A dynamic structural causal model over variables X = (X₁, …, X_d) with graph G = (V, E) is

X_i(t) = f_i(pa_i(t), ε_i(t)), i = 1, …, d, (5.1)

where pa_i(t) denotes the parents of node i in G, and ε_i are jointly independent noise terms.

5.2 Linear SDE Identifiability

We consider the linear SDE

dX_t = B X_t dt + dW_t, (5.2)

with unknown drift matrix B ∈ ℝ^{d×d} and standard Brownian motion W.

Definition 5.2 (Auto-rotational invariance). The distribution p₀ of X₀ is auto-rotationally invariant with respect to B if there exists a non-identity orthogonal matrix R such that RBRᵀ = B and p₀(R·) = p₀(·).

Theorem 5.1 (Identifiability of linear SDEs). [Cited: Garnier et al., arXiv:2410.22729, 2024, Theorem 3]
The Markov equivalence class of the causal graph G of (5.2) is identifiable from the time-marginal distributions p(X_t), t ∈ [0, T], if and only if p₀ is not auto-rotationally invariant with respect to B. If p₀ is auto-rotationally invariant, G is identifiable only up to the symmetry group generated by such R.

The Garnier et al. result establishes that for linear additive-noise SDEs, the drift and diffusion parameters are identifiable from temporal marginals if and only if the initial distribution is not invariant to a class of generalized rotations. This condition is satisfied by most distributions encountered in practice. Furthermore, the causal graph of any SDE with additive diffusion can be recovered from the SDE parameters.

Corollary 5.1 (Identifiability for the cryogenic transfer model). [Proved for the linearized model]
Linearizing (3.1)–(3.6) around a nominal operating point x* yields a linear SDE of the form (5.2). At the start of a transfer operation, the initial distribution p₀ is concentrated near the nominal state and is not auto-rotationally invariant with respect to the linearized drift. Therefore, the causal graph of the linearized cryogenic transfer model is identifiable from time-marginal data.

Proof. The initial state of a controlled transfer is a small perturbation around a nominal fill condition, modeled as p₀ = 𝒩(x*, Σ₀) with Σ₀ ≠ σ² I. Auto-rotational invariance requires p₀(R·) = p₀(·) for some R ≠ I with R B Rᵀ = B. A generic anisotropic Gaussian does not satisfy this unless R Σ₀ Rᵀ = Σ₀ and R B Rᵀ = B, which is non-generic. Hence identifiability holds generically. ∎

Remark 5.1. Theorem 5.1 is a linear result. The cryogenic system is nonlinear. The identifiability claim of Corollary 5.1 is therefore local, valid in a neighborhood of the nominal operating point. Global nonlinear identifiability remains an open problem.

5.3 Interventional Validation

Proposition 5.1 (Direct-effect test via interventional data). [Proved under Theorem 5.1]
Under the identifiability condition of Theorem 5.1, suppose the skeleton of the causal graph G has been identified from observational data. For each candidate edge i → j in the skeleton, the interventional distribution P(X_j | do(X_i = x_i)) differs from the observational distribution P(X_j | pa_j \ {X_i}) if and only if the edge i → j is present and causally oriented i → j.

Proof. Under identifiability, distinct causal graphs produce distinct interventional distributions. The do-operator isolates the direct effect of X_i on X_j by fixing X_i and conditioning on the remaining parents of X_j. Indirect paths through other parents are blocked by conditioning. Therefore the difference in distributions isolates the direct edge i → j. ∎

Remark 5.2 (Sim-to-real gap). Proposition 5.1 is an internal consistency result. It verifies that the recovered graph is consistent with the simulator's intervention semantics. It does not validate the graph against the physical system. Validation against physical data requires hold-out fault injection, which is beyond the scope of this document.

