Chapter 11. Conclusion

This document consolidates the mathematical foundation of the CSE-LLM architecture for cryogenic propellant transfer diagnosis. Its contributions are:

· A function-observability theorem for the vapor fraction, with an explicit sensor-augmentation corollary.
· A precise citation and hypothesis verification for MHE exponential convergence under joint i-IOSS, including an explicit persistency-of-excitation condition.
· A constructive bounded-weight lemma for a regularized unscented particle filter.
· A precise citation of the linear SDE identifiability condition and its interpretation for the cryogenic case.
· A formal statement of the causal compression hypothesis as an empirical proposition.
· A rejection-based safety envelope for the LLM reasoning layer, with an explicit non-claim about diagnostic truth.
· A prospective falsification protocol and a list of open problems.

Every result is classified as proved, cited, or hypothesized. No empirical claim is made. The central scientific question — whether the LLM layer adds measurable diagnostic value beyond the non-LLM envelope — is isolated as a testable hypothesis Δ_LLM > 0.

The philosophical position of this work is:

Prove what the non-LLM envelope guarantees. Measure what the LLM adds.

This posture is supported by the published literature: MHE convergence under i-IOSS and PE (Schiller & Müller, 2023), L² particle filter convergence (Del Moral, 2004), linear SDE causal identifiability (Garnier et al., 2024), and physics-constrained LLM fault diagnosis (Onnes, 2026). The remaining empirical program — observability atlases, compression-gap measurement, constraint-completeness stress tests, and paired Δ_LLM evaluation — constitutes the core of the future work.

