Appendix A. Proof of Theorem 4.1 (Function Observability of Vapor Fraction)

We give a complete proof of the function-observability condition for the vapor fraction \alpha_i of the cryogenic transfer system (3.1)–(3.6). The proof proceeds by computing the observability codistribution \mathcal{O}(x^*) and verifying the inclusion d\alpha_i \in \mathcal{O}(x^*).

A.1 Lie Derivatives of the Output Map

The output map is

h(x) = [P_1, T_1, P_2, T_2, \dot{m}_{12}]^\top. \tag{A.1}

Its differential is the constant matrix

dh(x) = \begin{bmatrix} e_1^\top \\ e_2^\top \\ e_5^\top \\ e_6^\top \\ e_9^\top \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{5 \times 11}, \tag{A.2}

where e_j is the j-th standard basis vector of \mathbb{R}^{11}. Thus dh(x) spans the directions corresponding to P_1, T_1, P_2, T_2, \dot{m}_{12} in the state space.

The first Lie derivative of h along the vector field f is

L_f h(x) = \frac{\partial h}{\partial x} f(x) = [\dot{P}_1, \dot{T}_1, \dot{P}_2, \dot{T}_2, \ddot{m}_{12}]^\top. \tag{A.3}

The terms \dot{P}_i and \dot{T}_i are obtained by differentiating the equation of state P_i = \mathcal{E}(m_i, T_i, \alpha_i, V_i) along trajectories:

\dot{P}_i = \left.\frac{\partial \mathcal{E}}{\partial m_i}\right|_{\alpha_i, T_i} \dot{m}_i
+ \left.\frac{\partial \mathcal{E}}{\partial \alpha_i}\right|_{m_i, T_i} \dot{\alpha}_i
+ \left.\frac{\partial \mathcal{E}}{\partial T_i}\right|_{m_i, \alpha_i} \dot{T}_i. \tag{A.4}

Similarly, the temperature dynamics \dot{T}_i are obtained from the energy balance (3.4). Writing the internal energy as u_i = u_i(m_i, T_i, \alpha_i), we have

\frac{d}{dt}(m_i u_i) = \dot{m}_i u_i + m_i \left( \frac{\partial u_i}{\partial m_i} \dot{m}_i
+ \frac{\partial u_i}{\partial T_i} \dot{T}_i
+ \frac{\partial u_i}{\partial \alpha_i} \dot{\alpha}_i \right). \tag{A.5}

Substituting the energy balance (3.4) and solving for \dot{T}_i yields

\dot{T}_i = \frac{1}{m_i \frac{\partial u_i}{\partial T_i}} \left[ \sum_j \dot{m}_{ji} h_j - \sum_j \dot{m}_{ij} h_i + Q_i - \dot{m}_i u_i - m_i \left( \frac{\partial u_i}{\partial m_i} \dot{m}_i + \frac{\partial u_i}{\partial \alpha_i} \dot{\alpha}_i \right) \right]. \tag{A.6}

The crucial observation is that \dot{T}_i depends on \alpha_i through the term involving \partial u_i / \partial \alpha_i and through \dot{\alpha}_i in (3.6). In the saturated two-phase regime, the latent-heat contribution to the internal energy is \partial u_i / \partial \alpha_i = -h_{fg}, where h_{fg} is the latent heat of vaporization. Thus

\frac{\partial \dot{T}_i}{\partial \alpha_i} = -\frac{h_{fg}}{m_i c_v} \neq 0, \tag{A.7}

where c_v = \partial u_i / \partial T_i is the specific heat at constant volume.

A.2 Observability Codistribution

The observability codistribution at x^* is

\mathcal{O}(x^*) = \operatorname{span}\{ dh(x^*), dL_f h(x^*), \ldots, dL_f^{10} h(x^*) \}. \tag{A.8}

We are interested in whether d\alpha_i(x^*) \in \mathcal{O}(x^*). Since d\alpha_i = e_{4}^\top for i=1 and e_{8}^\top for i=2, we need to check whether the fourth (or eighth) standard basis vector lies in the span of the rows of the observability matrix.

From dh(x^*), we obtain the rows e_1^\top, e_2^\top, e_5^\top, e_6^\top, e_9^\top. The row e_2^\top corresponds to dT_1, and e_6^\top to dT_2. The Lie derivative L_f h contributes rows corresponding to d\dot{P}_1, d\dot{T}_1, d\dot{P}_2, d\dot{T}_2, d\ddot{m}_{12}. The differential d\dot{T}_i contains a component along d\alpha_i given by (A.7):

d\dot{T}_i = \left( \frac{\partial \dot{T}_i}{\partial \alpha_i} \right) d\alpha_i + \text{(terms in other directions)}. \tag{A.9}

Since \partial \dot{T}_i / \partial \alpha_i \neq 0 in the saturated regime, the row d\dot{T}_i has a non-zero entry in the \alpha_i coordinate. Therefore, e_{4}^\top (or e_8^\top) can be expressed as a linear combination of dT_i and d\dot{T}_i and other rows already in \mathcal{O}(x^*). Hence

d\alpha_i \in \mathcal{O}(x^*). \tag{A.10}

In the single-phase regime, \partial \mathcal{E} / \partial \alpha_i \neq 0, so d\alpha_i appears directly in d\dot{P}_i from (A.4). Thus the inclusion holds a fortiori. This completes the proof of Theorem 4.1. \square

