Appendix B. Proof of Corollary 4.1 (Sensor Augmentation by Heater Probe)

We prove that a known heat pulse Q_{\text{probe}}(t) applied to tank i renders \alpha_i observable during a saturated two-phase interval, provided \partial P_{\text{sat}}/\partial T \neq 0.

The energy balance (3.4) with the probe becomes

\frac{d}{dt}(m_i u_i) = \sum_j \dot{m}_{ji} h_j - \sum_j \dot{m}_{ij} h_i + Q_i + Q_{\text{probe}}(t). \tag{B.1}

Under saturation, P_i = P_{\text{sat}}(T_i). The pressure transient is

\frac{dP_i}{dt} = \frac{\partial P_{\text{sat}}}{\partial T} \frac{dT_i}{dt}. \tag{B.2}

From (A.6), with Q_i replaced by Q_i + Q_{\text{probe}}, the temperature derivative becomes

\dot{T}_i = \frac{1}{m_i c_v} \left[ \sum_j \dot{m}_{ji} h_j - \sum_j \dot{m}_{ij} h_i + Q_i + Q_{\text{probe}} - \dot{m}_i u_i - m_i \frac{\partial u_i}{\partial m_i} \dot{m}_i + m_i h_{fg} \dot{\alpha}_i \right]. \tag{B.3}

The term m_i h_{fg} \dot{\alpha}_i depends on \alpha_i through \dot{\alpha}_i from (3.6). Therefore,

\frac{\partial \dot{T}_i}{\partial \alpha_i} = \frac{h_{fg}}{c_v} \frac{\partial \dot{\alpha}_i}{\partial \alpha_i} + \frac{h_{fg}}{c_v} \frac{\dot{\alpha}_i}{m_i} \frac{\partial m_i}{\partial \alpha_i} + \cdots \neq 0, \tag{B.4}

where the leading term is non-zero because \dot{\alpha}_i = (\Gamma_{\text{evap},i} - \alpha_i \dot{m}_{\text{net},i})/m_i depends linearly on \alpha_i. Consequently,

\frac{\partial}{\partial \alpha_i} \left( \frac{dP_i}{dt} \right) = \frac{\partial P_{\text{sat}}}{\partial T} \frac{\partial \dot{T}_i}{\partial \alpha_i} \neq 0. \tag{B.5}

Thus the pressure transient carries information about \alpha_i, proving the corollary. \square

