Appendix C. Proof of Corollary 4.2 (Rank Loss under Sensor Dropout)

If the temperature sensor on tank i fails, the output map loses the component T_i. The differential dh(x) loses the row e_{2}^\top (for i=1) or e_{6}^\top (for i=2). Since dT_i was one of the rows in \mathcal{O}(x^*), the rank of the observability codistribution decreases by at least one:

\operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}_{\text{no } T_i}(x^*) \leq \operatorname{rank} \mathcal{O}(x^*) - 1. \tag{C.1}

If additionally the tank is saturated and no heater probe is available, the remaining measurements are P_i and \dot{m}_{12}. Under saturation, P_i = P_{\text{sat}}(T_i), so P_i and T_i are functionally dependent; the information about \alpha_i that was transmitted through the energy balance to T_i is no longer accessible because T_i is not measured. The only remaining route for \alpha_i to affect a measured output would be through P_i, but P_i is slaved to T_i, and T_i is not observed. Hence

d\alpha_i \notin \mathcal{O}_{\text{no } T_i, \text{sat}}(x^*). \tag{C.2}

Thus \alpha_i becomes structurally unobservable from the remaining sensors. \square

