Appendix D. Verification of MHE Hypotheses (H1)–(H3)

We verify the hypotheses of Theorem 4.2 (Schiller & Müller, 2023) for the cryogenic transfer model.

D.1 Verification of (H1): Joint Incremental i-IOSS

Consider the joint state z = [x; \theta] where \theta = [Q_{\text{leak}}, a]^\top are the constant parameters. The joint dynamics are

\dot{z} = \begin{bmatrix} f(x, u, \theta) \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix} w \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}. \tag{D.1}

Schiller & Müller (2023) show that a joint i-IOSS Lyapunov function exists if and only if the state is incrementally detectable and the parameters satisfy a uniform persistency-of-excitation condition. For the linearized system around a nominal operating point, the state dynamics are locally exponentially stable under (S1)–(S3). The parameter modes are not contractive without excitation. Therefore, (H1) holds locally provided that the PE condition (H2) is satisfied.

D.2 Verification of (H2): Persistency of Excitation

The measurement matrix H = \partial h / \partial x has full row rank 5 in the single-phase regime. In the saturated two-phase regime, P_i and T_i are functionally related, and the rank drops to at most 4 per tank. However, the PE condition (4.7) requires that the input u(t) excite all modes of the joint state–parameter system. For a cryogenic transfer operation with time-varying mass flow \dot{m}_{12}(t) and periodic heater-probe pulses Q_{\text{probe}}(t), the input is sufficiently rich to excite both the state and parameter modes. Specifically, the mass flow variations excite the mass and pressure modes, while the heater pulses excite the thermal and phase-change modes. The acceleration a is excited by the vehicle dynamics. Therefore, there exist c_1, c_2 > 0 and T_{\text{PE}} \geq N such that (4.7) holds for generic trajectories. This condition must be verified for the specific input profile used in any experimental campaign.

D.3 Verification of (H3): Arrival-Cost Regularization

The arrival cost is computed by the extended Kalman filter (EKF) on the horizon's terminal state. The EKF covariance update is

P_{k+1} = (I - K_k H) P_k (I - K_k H)^\top + K_k R K_k^\top, \tag{D.2}

where K_k is the Kalman gain. Since R \succ 0 and P_0 \succ 0, the covariance remains positive definite. The arrival cost is then

\Gamma_k(\hat{x}) = \|\hat{x} - \bar{x}_k\|_{P_k^{-1}}^2 - c_\Gamma, \tag{D.3}

which satisfies (H3) with c_\Gamma a bounded constant.

