Appendix E. Proof of Lemma 4.1 (Bounded Importance Weights)

We prove that the regularized proposal (4.12) yields bounded importance weights on any compact set K \subset \mathbb{R}^{n_x}.

By (S2), the product p(y_t \mid x_t) p(x_t \mid x_{t-1}) is continuous and hence bounded on the compact set K \times K \times \mathcal{Y}, where \mathcal{Y} is the compact range of y_t. Thus there exists M_{\text{prod}} < \infty such that

p(y_t \mid x_t) p(x_t \mid x_{t-1}) \leq M_{\text{prod}} \quad \forall (x_{t-1}, x_t, y_t) \in K \times K \times \mathcal{Y}. \tag{E.1}

The regularized proposal is

q_\epsilon(x_t \mid x_{t-1}, y_t) = (1 - \epsilon) q_{\text{UKF}}(x_t \mid \cdot) + \epsilon \mathcal{N}(x_t; \mu_{\text{UKF}}, \Sigma_{\text{UKF}} + \sigma_\epsilon^2 I). \tag{E.2}

The second term is a Gaussian density with full support on \mathbb{R}^{n_x}. Its minimum on the compact set K is strictly positive:

\inf_{x_t \in K} \mathcal{N}(x_t; \mu_{\text{UKF}}, \Sigma_{\text{UKF}} + \sigma_\epsilon^2 I) \geq m_{\text{Gauss}} > 0. \tag{E.3}

Since \epsilon > 0, we have

q_\epsilon(x_t \mid x_{t-1}, y_t) \geq \epsilon \, m_{\text{Gauss}} > 0 \quad \forall (x_{t-1}, x_t) \in K \times K. \tag{E.4}

Therefore,

\frac{p(y_t \mid x_t) p(x_t \mid x_{t-1})}{q_\epsilon(x_t \mid x_{t-1}, y_t)} \leq \frac{M_{\text{prod}}}{\epsilon \, m_{\text{Gauss}}} =: M_\epsilon < \infty. \tag{E.5}

This proves the lemma. \square

