Appendix J. Derivation of the Equation of State and Regime Distinction

The equation of state is written generically as

P_i = \mathcal{E}(m_i, T_i, \alpha_i, V_i). \tag{J.1}

In the single-phase regime (subcooled liquid or superheated vapor), the pressure depends on \alpha_i through the mixture density. For a homogeneous mixture,

\rho_i = \alpha_i \rho_v + (1 - \alpha_i) \rho_l, \tag{J.2}

where \rho_v and \rho_l are the vapor and liquid densities. Since m_i = \rho_i V_i, we have

P_i = \mathcal{E}\left( \frac{m_i}{V_i}, T_i, \alpha_i \right), \tag{J.3}

and \partial \mathcal{E}/\partial \alpha_i \neq 0.

In the saturated two-phase regime, the pressure is the saturation pressure:

P_i = P_{\text{sat}}(T_i). \tag{J.4}

Thus \partial \mathcal{E}/\partial \alpha_i = 0. However, the temperature dynamics still depend on \alpha_i through the latent-heat term in the energy balance, as shown in Appendix A. This is the key distinction that drives Theorem 4.1.

